+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions instead of field-aware merge
+
+## Context
+
+Two tablets can hold edits to the same visit for days: both fetched the same visit state, both workers corrected it offline, and both submissions eventually reach the server. The record store has to turn those submissions into one current record without treating arrival order as authority and without discarding a submitted edit.
+
+The mechanism chosen here sets the record-store submission contract, so the two nodes downstream of it are sized around the result. Tablet-sync decides what a tablet stores and what it sends; review-export decides what coordinators examine and which records may enter the Friday CSV. The two candidate mechanisms move effort to different places: one moves work into a review queue coordinators can see, count, and staff; the other moves work into merge rules that run unseen on every record.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No submitted edit is discarded.** Every payload that passes validation is retained and reachable in submission history, whatever becomes of it.
+- **Combined output stays traceable.** Any value reaching the current record must resolve back to the submissions it came from (tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*).
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific integrity of automatically produced values (major).** Can the mechanism produce a value in the current record that no worker or coordinator submitted? Derived from the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* extended to silent *combination*: a discarded edit is noticed when someone looks for it; a fused edit looks like data.
+- **Maintenance burden on the two-coordinator team (major).** Derived from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries are logic that must track field evolution; a comparison screen is operated but not maintained as logic.
+- **Review volume before the Friday export (moderate).** How many of the ~6,000 visits per season reach a coordinator's queue even when the concurrent edits touched unrelated fields. Cuts against whole-visit revisions.
+- **Submission size and tablet storage (moderate).** Complete visit payloads per submission versus base snapshots plus structured changes, on 12 shared tablets holding multi-day drafts.
+- **Legibility of a conflict to a coordinator (moderate).** How readily a human can judge two complete records against each other, versus judge whether an automated field combination is scientifically right.
+- **Recovery when the mechanism is wrong (moderate).** If the mechanism errs, how does anyone find out, and what does it cost to undo? A wrong automatic combination is already the current record when someone notices; a wrong resolution is re-resolvable and never entered the record unattended.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission carries the complete visit payload plus the ID of the revision it started from (its base revision). A submission whose base is the visit's current revision extends the record normally. A submission whose base is any older revision is stored as a competing complete revision and the visit is marked unresolved; a coordinator settles the competing revisions by submitting an outcome payload that names the contributions it resolves. The store never parses the payload's fields, and nothing combines two submissions by code.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers fetch visit V at revision 7 and correct it offline. The first submission arrives with base 7, becomes revision 8, and V stays resolved. The second arrives with base 7: it is stored as a competing revision, V is flagged unresolved, and revision 8 remains the standing state until a coordinator compares the two records and submits a resolution naming both submissions.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Scientific integrity — **strong.** Every value in a current record was submitted whole by a worker or authored by a named coordinator; the server never fuses fields.
+- Maintenance burden — **strong.** Acceptance rules compare one base revision ID; there are no per-field or per-list merge rules to extend when fields change.
+- Review volume — **weak.** Unrelated concurrent edits (one worker corrects an observer name, another adds a missing species) still collide and queue, because the store cannot tell related from unrelated without parsing the payload.
+- Submission size and tablet storage — **partial.** Each submission repeats the full record, so payloads and retries are heavier than a diff; but a tablet draft is already a complete record, so local storage needs no snapshot-plus-delta machinery.
+- Legibility of a conflict — **strong.** Coordinators compare two complete records against the visit's context, the same reading they already do with field notes.
+- Recovery when wrong — **strong.** The failure mode is a queue item a human got wrong, re-resolvable from intact history; the mechanism itself has no silent failure.
+
+**Summary.** Correctness moves into a visible queue, and that queue absorbs every concurrent edit.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries its base state plus structured changes rather than a complete record. The server applies maintained merge rules per field kind — scalars, observer-list entries, species entries — combines changes that touch disjoint fields, and queues only overlapping ones for review. Review-export carries field-level provenance for every merged value and a review view for the overlaps that did queue.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Scientific integrity — **partial.** Rules that see fields independently can combine edits that are disjoint by field name and related in fact — one worker revising a species identification while another revises that species' cover estimate, for instance — producing combinations no one reviewed. Provenance records where a value came from; it does not make the combination right.
+- Maintenance burden — **weak.** Every field kind needs rule logic; new or restructured fields extend the rule set; the rules are owned by the same two coordinators, with no systems administrator to lean on.
+- Review volume — **strong.** Only genuinely overlapping changes queue, so most visits reach the export with no human touch.
+- Submission size and tablet storage — **partial.** Tablets retain base snapshots and send structured changes, which is more state to manage across restarts and 48-hour disconnections than one complete draft record.
+- Legibility of a conflict — **partial.** A queued overlap arrives after the server has already assembled surrounding fields, so coordinators judge a partly-assembled record rather than two submitted ones.
+- Recovery when wrong — **weak.** An incorrect combination is the current record before anyone is asked; finding it later means auditing provenance rather than reading a queue.
+
+**Summary.** Less visible work, in exchange for logic that has to be right on every record.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The mechanism wins both major criteria: no value enters a current record without a worker or coordinator submitting it whole, and no merge-rule maintenance lands on a team that runs without a dedicated systems administrator. What it concedes is the moderate criterion it loses — review volume — and that cost is the acceptable one here: a queue is visible, countable, and staffable, while a rule that fuses two scientifically related edits is neither. The decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and is taken against the defensible lean that more visits should reach the export unattended.
+
+**What committing forecloses.** Automatic combination of any kind: concurrent edits to unrelated fields consume coordinator attention even though a rule could have merged them silently. The field-aware approach is not rejected as a direction — it is deferred. The choice is worth reopening when the queue outgrows what two coordinators can clear before Friday (review volume becoming a staffing problem rather than a warning), or when merge rules for observer lists and species entries could be maintained and trusted by this team, which removes the two weaknesses that sank it here.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- *Tablet-sync* retains the base revision ID with every local draft, submits complete visit payloads under idempotency keys, sizes local storage for whole-record drafts and their retries, and surfaces pending competing revisions to the worker rather than only the standing revision.
+- *Review-export* builds a whole-record revision comparison for coordinators, gates export eligibility on the record store's unresolved-work indicator (unresolved visits stay out of the CSV until settled), and carries resolution provenance so an exported value still traces to the submissions behind it.
