+## Revision Model
+
+Each visit is a chain of revisions. A revision is a complete visit payload together with its author, server receipt time, and the revision it was based on.
+
+- A submission with no base revision creates the visit and its first revision.
+- A submission whose base revision is the visit's current revision becomes the current revision.
+- A submission whose base revision is any other revision the visit holds is stored as a competing revision and the visit becomes unresolved. The current revision does not change.
+- A submission naming a base revision the store does not hold for that visit is rejected without storing a submission.
+
+While a visit is unresolved, its current revision remains the standing state returned by retrieval, and its competing revisions remain retrievable for comparison. No accepted submission is discarded, rewritten, or removed by later activity.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves unresolved work by submitting a resolution: a complete visit payload together with the IDs of the submissions it settles. The resolution records the coordinator's identity and its contributing submission IDs, and its payload becomes the current revision. A resolution may settle some of a visit's competing submissions and leave others pending, in which case the visit stays unresolved. The submissions a resolution settles remain in history unchanged.
+
+A resolution payload may adopt one contributing submission's record or carry a record the coordinator entered, provided it names the submissions it settles.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolution payload.
+
+A retry with the same idempotency key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
+
+Current-state retrieval returns the current revision's payload and revision ID, plus an indicator of whether unresolved competing revisions exist for that visit. Submission-history retrieval returns every stored submission with its author, server receipt time, base revision, resulting revision, and disposition (current, competing, or settled).
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency mechanism for independently edited visits | Whole-visit revisions: submissions carry a base revision ID and a complete payload; a non-current base yields a competing revision settled by a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge: submissions carry base state plus structured changes; disjoint field changes combine automatically, overlaps queue for review | Whole-visit revisions keep every value in a current record attributable to a named worker or coordinator and add no merge-rule maintenance for a two-coordinator team; the cost — every concurrent edit queues for review, even edits to unrelated fields — is visible and staffable, whereas a merge rule that fuses scientifically related edits fails unseen. Full reasoning, including the conditions for revisiting the choice: [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md). |
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+### Resolved
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
